  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: The large upper level ridge over the four \ncorners region will continue to remain in its location for the next \nday or two. This will keep a fairly feeble southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow aloft across the forecast area. Models are \nhinting at a weak upper level trough developing along the western \ncoast of the United States. A weak southwest flow is expected that \nwill shunt some of the subtropical moisture east, especially over \nNorthern Nevada. This will bring a decrease in the convective \nactivity across the north, though activity is expected to persist in \nCentral Nevada as evident by the decreasing precipitable water \nvalues in the north. Highs in the north will be a degree or three \ncooler with readings in the low to upper 90s expected over the area. \nWinds will be breezy especially over the north with gusts 25 to 30 \nmph. Isolated to scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms are \nexpected mainly across the US Highway 50 corridor with some activity \nreaching Southern Elko county. Main threats will be the cloud to \nground lightning strikes and wind gusts near 55 mph. Look for \nactivity to gradually wane during the overnight hours with lows in \nthe 50s and 60s.\n\nBy Tuesday, all models show the slow strengthening of the upper\ntrough across the western United States with the four corners\nridge maintaining its position. Convective activity will again be\nconfined to those locales along the US Highway 50 corridor.\nAfternoon high temperatures are expected to again cool by several\ndegrees with readings in the low to mid 90s. Winds will be breezy\nduring the afternoon with gusts of 20 to 25 mph. Overnight lows\nwill be in the 50s. \n\nOn Wednesday, a weak disturbance is expected to push across Northern \nand Central Nevada during afternoon and evening. This looks like it \nwill lead to an uptick in the convective activity as guidance is \nshowing a fifty to seventy percent chance of showers and \nthunderstorms during this time frame. WPC is highlighting this time \nwith an area expansion northward of the excessive rainfall outlook. \nWill need to continue looking at this event as it unfolds for \nmoderate to locally brief heavy rainfall. Storm motions at this time \nare forecast to be around seven to 14 knots. Highs in the afternoon \nwill further cool with readings in the 80s and 90s. Shower activity \nwill continue into the overnight with lows in the 50s.\n\nThursday through Sunday, models continue to show a daily pattern\nof shower and thunderstorm activity with high temperatures\ncontinuing to cool. Readings are expected to drop into the 80s by\nnext weekend with lows in the 50s.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: Confidence is high regarding a \ncooling trend Tuesday through Friday, as well as afternoon and \nevening thunderstorms in Central Nevada. Confidence is currently \nmoderate for Wednesday's convective development. No deviations from \nthe NBM model output.",
